{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses answered, etc.</b> One must understand what place there is for the argument ״they will not believe״ after HaShem promised him and said ״and they will listen to your voice.״ Further, why did he double the phrasing, saying ״they will not believe and they will not hear my voice״? And one could possibly say that since a person has free choice and will, he did not trust them to act faithfully. And his saying <b>and they will not hear</b>, etc., means even hearing his voice they would not want to hear. But this approach does not suffice for the verse's intent, for it was not for him to harbor thoughts after the attributes of HaShem blessed be He and to suspect Israel so much. There is further a fine point to examine in his saying <b>הֵן</b> (״if״) and not saying ״אִם לֹא״ (״if not״), etc." "And it may be explained correctly by way of what Rambam wrote in chapter 7 of Hilkhot Yesodei ha-Torah, and this is his language: Prophecy rests only upon a great sage, wise, etc., whole in body, etc., end quote. And the Kesef Mishneh wrote, and this is his language: This requires investigation — why did he not write that one must be wealthy, mighty, and humble? And he answered that those conditions are required for one who prophesies on a permanent basis, but Rambam did not descend to explain except regarding one who prophesies not on a permanent basis. Now, even if we were to say that this prophecy of Moses were occasional — which it is not — even so he advances an argument that is plausible given his humility, that he lacks the conditions by virtue of which a prophet is believed by Israel, and that is his saying <b>וְהֵן</b> — meaning, and thus it is the case that they will not believe me, for I am not a great sage in wisdom and full in all the requisite conditions; and needless to say if it is reckoned as permanent prophecy — as is indeed the truth, for he comes and goes from HaShem to Israel and to Pharaoh and to Egypt — if so, he must be wealthy, which was not the case. And this is your sign: he was shepherding his father-in-law's flocks and did not become wealthy until the wilderness, from the tablets (Nedarim 38a). If so, if Israel come to ask their elders for judgment — הֵן, they will not believe me according to the law. \n{n: 'In the final clause, reading לִשְׁאוֹל לְזִקְנֵיהֶם הַמִּשְׁפָּט literally as \"to ask their elders the judgment,\" possibly alluding to the legal requirement that a prophet be examined by the elders; the translation preserves the hyper-literal construction.'}" "And what he said — <b>and they will not hear my voice</b> — here he alluded to yet another plausible argument, for he is heavy of mouth, as he later explicitly revealed in the explanation ״for I am heavy of mouth.״ And this matter is one of the conditions of a prophet, even an occasional one: that he be whole in body. And this is one of the bodily deficiencies, and therefore he said ״and they will not hear״ on account of his voice, for he is heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue. And he stated the reason: <b>for they will say, He has not appeared to you</b>, etc., meaning, you are not worthy that He should appear to you at all, even occasionally. And the fact that Moses did not advance this argument until now, and only asked ״and they will say to me, What is His name? What shall I say?״ — this indicates that he was not concerned that they would not believe, for had he been concerned about this he would not have pursued knowledge of the Name, which is something for which the answer would immediately be said to him: ״This is My name.״" "<b>Or perhaps</b> in his first question, ״and they will say,״ etc., he intended that HaShem would reveal His Name and its sod, through which He would perform wonders — for the explicit Name performs wonders, and through its mediation one acquires all acquisitions, both tangible excellences and excellences of fortune and wisdom, and thereby perfects oneself to be worthy of prophecy, in the sod of (Psalms 91:14) ״אֲשַׂגְּבֵהוּ כִּי יָדַע שְׁמִי״ (״I will set him on high, for he has known My name״). It would also perform wonders in their eyes through the wondrous paths of HaShem. And in HaShem's response to him, it is possible that He did not inform him of the sodot of the Name and its properties and the wonders He would perform through them; therefore he returned and argued ״הֵן,״ etc., as I have explained." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that Moses was not prompted to advance this argument, ״they will not believe,״ except because HaShem said to him something new that he had not heard before, namely His saying ״and I know that the king of Egypt will not let you go, and not with a strong hand.״ He said: From now on you have forfeited the assurance that Pharaoh will regard his mission and send them. If so, ״they will not believe me,״ and when the mission drags on and he does not send them, they will further refuse to hear my voice. And the reason is <b>for they will say, He has not appeared to you</b>, etc. — for if these things were from El Elyon, who is this who would refuse to do His bidding and not have His betrayer-in-the-wall struck down (I Kings 14:10)? Rather, surely HaShem did not send you, and you did not even attain prophecy."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:35.158240436Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}